<TopicPage slug="subjects/statistics/foundational/probability">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Probability puts a number between 0 and 1 on uncertainty: 0 is impossible, 1 is certain,
    0.5 is a fair coin. It is the mathematics of not-knowing — and modern life runs on it:
    weather forecasts, insurance, medical screening, bank risk models, queue design. The rules
    are few; the discipline is in applying them without fooling yourself.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the three rules and one worked example" widget="equationStep" config={probCfg} />

  <h2>Expected value: the casino's compass</h2>
  <p>
    Multiply each outcome by its probability and sum. A £1 British lottery ticket: about a 1-in-14
    million jackpot chance and a roof of smaller prizes — expected return near 50p. Play forever
    and you lose half a pound per play, on average. Casinos, insurers and lotteries all live in
    the gap between their expected value and yours.
  </p>

  <h2>Where intuition fails</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The gambler's fallacy.</strong> Ten heads in a row leaves the next toss at exactly 50/50 — coins have no memory. (Ten heads <em>does</em> make "this coin is rigged" more likely; those are different questions.)</li>
    <li><strong>Conjunction errors.</strong> "Linda is a bank teller" is more probable than "Linda is a bank teller <em>and</em> a feminist" — adding detail can only shrink probability, yet most people rank it higher (Tversky & Kahneman, 1983).</li>
    <li><strong>The law of large numbers</strong> (Jacob Bernoulli, 1713) is the honest hope: averages settle down over many trials. It does <em>not</em> mean short runs must "even out".</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One vocabulary note: the <strong>frequentist</strong> reading defines probability as long-run
    frequency; the <strong>Bayesian</strong> reading treats it as degree of belief, updated by
    evidence (Laplace formalised much of it in 1812). Same rules, different philosophy — and the
    split still runs through modern statistics.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Bernoulli, Ars Conjectandi (1713); Laplace, Théorie analytique des probabilités (1812); Tversky & Kahneman, Psychological Review (1983).</p>
</TopicPage>
